C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      Example 2.1 from Bishop Et Al Text (https://digitalcommons.usu.edu/ecstatic_all/76/)
      Modifies Example to add a labor constraint
       
      and further modified to represent the vehicle production problem with metal, curcuit boards, and labor consraints
       
      THE PROBLEM:
       
      How many coups and minivans should a car manufacturer produce in a year?
       
      The data are as follows:
       
      •   The car manufacturer profits by $6,000 per coup produced and $7,000 per minivan produced.
      •   Each coup requires 1,000 pounds of metal to produce while each minivan requires 2,000 pounds of metal.
      •   The manufacturer has 4,000,000 pounds of metal available for the year.
      •   Coups have more electronics and features. Thus, each coup requires 4 circuit boards per vehicle while each minivan requires 3 circuit boards per vehicle.
      •   The manufacturer has purchased 12,000 circuit boards for the year.
      •   One coup takes one worker 5 days to produce while one worker takes 2.5 days to produce a minivan. 
      •   The manufacturer has 67 workers and runs their factory for 261 days per year (i.e., the manufacturer has 17,500 worker-days per year).   
       
      Determine the number of coups and minivans to produce
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 15, 2015
  31   
  32  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  33  SETS plnt crops growing /Eggplant, Tomatoes/
  34           res resources /Water, Land, Labor/
  35  * for car production problem
  36          cars Cars to produce  / Coups, Minivans  /
  37          materials Materials used to produce vehicles /Metal, CircuitBoards, Labor/;
  38   
  39  * 2. DEFINE input data
  40  PARAMETERS
  41     c(plnt) Objective function coefficients ($ per plant)
  42           /Eggplant 6,
  43          Tomatoes 7 /
  44   
  45     b(res) Right hand constraint values (per resource)
  46            /Water 4000000,
  47             Land  12000,
  48             Labor  17500/
  49   
  50      carprofit(cars) Profit per car produced ($ per car)
  51          / coups 6
  52          minivans 7/
  53   
  54      carconstraints(materials) Righthand constraint values for car production (per resource)
  55          /Metal 4000000,
  56          CircuitBoards 12000,
  57          Labor 17500/;
  58   
  59  TABLE A(plnt,res) Left hand side constraint coefficients
  60                   Water    Land   Labor
  61   Eggplant        1000      4       5
  62   Tomatoes        2000      3       2.5;
  63   
  64  TABLE Z(cars,materials) Left hand side constraint coefficients for the car problem
  65                        Metal        CircuitBoards         Labor
  66  *Coups         1000               4                   5
  67  * Minivans       2000               3                 2.5;
  68   Coups               1000                     4             5
  69   Minivans          2000                     3              2.5;
  70   
  71   
  72   
  73  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  74  VARIABLES X(plnt) plants planted (Number)
  75            VPROFIT  total profit ($);
  76   
  77  VARIABLES
  78            W(cars) cars to produce (Number)
  79          CPROFIT total profit from producing cars ($);
  80   
  81  * Non-negativity constraints
  82  POSITIVE VARIABLES X, W;
  83   
  84  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  85  EQUATIONS
  86     PROFIT Total profit ($) and objective function value
  87     RES_CONSTRAIN(res) Resource Constraints
  88     PROFITCARS Total profit from producing cars ($) the objective function value
  89     MATERIALCONSTRAINTS(materials) Resources used to produce cars;
  90   
  91  PROFIT..                 VPROFIT =E= SUM(plnt, c(plnt)*X(plnt));
  92  RES_CONSTRAIN(res) ..    SUM(plnt, A(plnt,res)*X(plnt)) =L= b(res);
  93   
  94  PROFITCARS..        CPROFIT =E=  SUM(cars, carprofit(cars)*W(cars));
  95  MATERIALCONSTRAINTS(materials).. SUM(cars, Z(cars,materials)*W(cars)) =L= carconstraints(materials);
  96   
  97  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  98  MODEL PLANTING /PROFIT, RES_CONSTRAIN/;
  99  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
 100  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
 101  MODEL CARPRODUCTION / PROFITCARS, MATERIALCONSTRAINTS/;
 102   
 103   
 104   
 105  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
 106  * Solve the PLANTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
 107  *     to maximize VPROFIT
 108  *SOLVE PLANTING USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
 109   
 110  SOLVE CARPRODUCTION USING LP MAXIMIZING CPROFIT;
 111   
 112  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


